title: 'pallet-revive: apply the EIP-150 63/64 rule to nested calls'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    When a contract calls another contract or a precompile, or creates a contract, the nested call
    now gets at most `63/64 * (r - s) + s`, with `r` the caller's remaining resources and `s` the
    call stipend (zero without one). In Substrate mode the 63/64 rule applies to each weight
    dimension and to the storage deposit. In Ethereum mode it applies to the gas and to the
    caller's weight limit, and also to the caller's storage deposit limit if it has one. The weight
    and storage deposit the nested call can use follow from these. A nested call that asks for more
    than its 63/64 share, or sets no limit, gets less than before, for example a Solidity `call`
    without a gas argument. The rule does not apply to the transaction's first call.

    The 63/64 rule also applies to calls to the System precompile, so `ISystem.weightLeft()` now
    returns the weight left without the part the calling contract keeps.

    As on Ethereum, the part the caller keeps lets Solidity contracts handle a callee that runs out
    of gas, for example with a `try`/`catch` around a call or contract creation, or with
    Multicall3's `aggregate3`, which continues after a failed call.

    In Ethereum mode, the weight part of a transaction's fee is the larger of the cost of its
    ref_time and the cost of its proof size. When the proof-size cost is the larger one, a caller
    with less gas left than the stipend's price can still pay for the stipend's weight. Its callee
    then gets all of the caller's gas, as before, and the caller keeps none.

    The dry-run estimates now account for the gas, weight and storage deposit each caller keeps:
    `weight_required` and `max_storage_deposit` of `ContractResult` and `EthTransactInfo`, and
    `EthTransactInfo::eth_gas`, include 1/63 of what each nested call needs beyond its stipend.
    So does the result of `eth_estimateGas`, which still suffices for the transaction to succeed.

    A dry run also no longer reports a `max_storage_deposit` that is too low when a contract
    frees storage and then makes a call that reenters it, or, in Substrate mode, when a call
    writes storage that an earlier call freed.

    An Ethereum dry run now runs the call with at most the maximum `eth_transact` weight minus the
    transaction's base weight, instead of the whole maximum, so that the weight it reports leaves
    room for the base weight also when a nested call uses up its share. A call that needs more
    weight than that now runs out of gas in the dry run, as it does in a transaction.

    A transaction can now fail in a nested call if its limits do not include the part each caller
    keeps. This applies to limits from a dry run on a runtime without this change, which must be
    estimated again, and to limits set to what a dry run or an earlier transaction consumed, for
    example a `weight_limit` set to `weight_consumed`, a `storage_deposit_limit` set to
    `storage_deposit`, or a gas limit set to a receipt's `gasUsed`. The limits should be set from
    a dry run's `weight_required`, `max_storage_deposit` and `eth_gas` fields instead, or from the
    result of `eth_estimateGas`.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `ResourceMeter::new_nested` now applies the 63/64 rule to the limits of the frame it creates
    when called on a `FrameMeter`, but not on a `TransactionMeter`. Calls and instantiations a
    precompile makes through `precompiles::Ext::call` and `precompiles::ExtWithInfo::instantiate`
    follow the rule too, including with `CallResources::NoLimits`.
    `ResourceMeter::weight_required` and `ResourceMeter::deposit_required`, which the dry runs
    report, now include the part each caller keeps. The public API does not change otherwise, the
    weights stay the same, and no storage migration is needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-uapi
  bump: none
